Atomic brand assets — licence
=============================

Copyright (c) 2020 Joep Meindertsma / Ontola.io. All rights reserved.

THE CONTENTS OF THIS DIRECTORY ARE NOT COVERED BY THE MIT LICENCE THAT
APPLIES TO THE REST OF THIS REPOSITORY.

The MIT licence in the repository root grants rights in the *software*. It
does not grant any right to use the names, logos, marks or other brand
features of Atomic Server, Atomic Data, Atomic Canvas or Ontola. Those are
reserved. This directory — the source marks in `src/`, the generated
artifacts in `dist/`, and every icon this package renders into the apps —
is licensed separately, on the terms below.

Nothing here restricts your rights under the MIT licence with respect to
the software itself. You may fork, modify, redistribute and sell the code.
What you may not do is present your version as though it were ours.


What you may do without asking
------------------------------

1. Use the marks to refer accurately to the Atomic projects — in articles,
   talks, documentation, comparison tables, "works with Atomic Server"
   integration notices, and similar factual reference.

2. Redistribute an *unmodified* build of the software with its icons in
   place. Packaging Atomic Server for a distro or app store is fine.

3. Reproduce the marks with their proportions and colours intact. Do not
   recolour, rotate, stretch, add effects, or rebuild them from memory —
   generate from `brand/src/`.


What requires written permission
--------------------------------

1. Using the marks on a *modified* build or a fork. If you change the
   software, replace the branding. This is the rule that keeps the marks
   meaningful, and it is deliberately the same one the Linux distributions
   apply to Firefox and Chromium.

2. Any use suggesting sponsorship, endorsement, affiliation or an official
   relationship that does not exist.

3. Use in your own product name, company name, domain name, app-store
   listing name, or logo.

4. Merchandise, or any commercial use of the marks as a selling feature.

Requests: joep@ontola.io


Why this carve-out exists
-------------------------

Atomic is free software and is meant to stay that way. Trademark is the
mechanism that lets it stay free: anyone may run, fork and sell the code,
while the *name* continues to denote software that actually comes from
this project. Reserving the marks is what makes a sustainable business
possible around an MIT codebase without ever having to restrict the code.

See TRADEMARKS.md in the repository root for the full policy.
